# TASK-260715-1ozsb6 results — review round 2 rework

## Delivered

- Added the named `ReluxTunnelLibSSH2Adapter` target as the only Swift target importing `ReluxLibSSH2`; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains candidate-neutral.
- Implemented the pinned static libssh2/OpenSSL session adapter: custom bounded send/receive callbacks, nonblocking EAGAIN progress, injected socket readiness, async external signing, pre-auth raw host-key policy, approved algorithm enforcement, public-key auth, direct-tcpip, exec, exec/stdin upload with concurrent drains, manual/byte/time client rekey, server-rekey protocol driving, forced keepalive global requests, typed privacy-safe errors, available metrics, allocator hooks, and bounded cleanup.
- Wired the factory into the macOS provider configuration and dependency-injected harness registration. The harness linkage smoke keeps the static candidate in the release link graph. iOS provider graphs remain unchanged per scope.
- Consumer receive-window credit, RFC channel-open reason taxonomy, exact exec-exit presence and `coreDumped`, and deep rekey/keepalive observability are explicitly `unsupported` and traced to `TASK-260728-3cveay`. No deferred value is inferred, hard-coded, or parsed from engine prose.

## Review round 2 rework

- Added a bounded FIFO gate around libssh2 state machines stored on the shared session. Channel-open, rekey, and reply-requiring global-request calls retain one permit across EAGAIN; independent channel payload reads and writes remain interleavable.
- Corrected the custom transport callback ABI to return `-EAGAIN` directly. Returning `-1` plus `errno=EAGAIN` had made normal socket backpressure appear as a disconnect to libssh2.
- Serialized socket progress behind one queued owner. Forced overlapping service calls prove exact-once partial writes and maximum socket-write concurrency of one.
- Made timeout adaptation structured: the losing task is cancelled and joined before the operation returns. Bridge shutdown marks the bridge terminal, closes the injected connection, cancels and joins every queued service task, then discards buffers and frees engine state. A deliberately non-cooperative late socket write regression proves the ordering.
- Tracked channel pointer users across suspension. One read per channel stream is enforced; queued writes reserve against the per-channel cap; cancel/reset/close marks a typed disposition, waits for pending users, and then frees.
- Applied the overall upload deadline and source chunk bound. External signer tasks are explicitly tracked, notification-driven, cancelled, and joined during teardown.
- Applied `allowedConsecutiveMisses` to automatic keepalive failures before connection-fatal teardown. Preserved the active connect phase before teardown and populated timeout/cancellation counters plus pending-open/read/write and queued-byte gauges.
- Added an ephemeral macOS loopback OpenSSH fixture with an in-memory P-256 external signer. It proves host-policy-before-credential ordering, public-key authentication, direct-tcpip echo, concurrent exec opens, exec/stdin upload, server-rekey traffic survival, manual client rekey, keepalive transmission, five connected read/cancel races, and three successful connect/close resource baselines.
- Focused coverage consists of 14 bridge/lifecycle tests plus one successful end-to-end adapter conformance test.

## Validation evidence

- `swift-format lint --recursive --strict Package.swift Sources Tests`: exit 0.
- `git diff --check`: exit 0.
- `swift test --filter LibSSH2BridgeTests`: exit 0; 14 tests passed.
- `swift test --filter LibSSH2AdapterIntegrationTests`: exit 0; the successful loopback M0 flow passed.
- `make validate-core`: exit 0; boundary/dependency checks, 353 tests in 31 suites, and `swift build` passed.
