   179	- canonical profile hostname and connected SSH endpoint;
   180	- exact negotiated host-key algorithm name;
   181	- exact SSH wire-format host-key bytes, copied into immutable `Data`;
   182	- canonical OpenSSH-style `SHA256:<base64-without-padding>` fingerprint computed
   183	  from those bytes;
   184	- lane identity and the profile's opaque trust-record reference.
   185	
   186	Evidence supports Ed25519, ECDSA, and RSA-SHA2 algorithm names without changing
   187	the public shape. Unknown algorithms remain representable and are rejectable.
   188	Raw bytes and the fingerprint are available to policy even when the decision is
   189	reject.
   190	
   250	`openExecChannel` opens an SSH session channel, sends exactly one exec request,
   251	and returns only after acceptance. It exposes stdin through `writeSome` and
   252	`finishWriting`, stdout through `read`, stderr through `readStandardError`, and
   253	exit metadata through `waitForExit`.
   254	
   255	Stdout and stderr have independent single-reader gates and bounded buffers.
   256	They may be drained concurrently with stdin writes. `SSHExecExit` distinguishes
   257	numeric status, signal, and `notReported`; a nonzero remote exit is a result, not
   258	a transport error. `waitForExit` returns only after exit metadata or remote
   259	close establishes that it is absent. Exit metadata may arrive before stream EOF,
   260	so callers may continue draining until each read returns `nil`.
   261	
   262	### 7.3 Exec-stdin upload
   263	
   264	`upload` is a convenience operation implemented only with an exec channel; it
   265	MUST NOT initialize or call SFTP. It:
   266	
   267	1. opens the caller-supplied, already safely constructed exec request;
   268	2. reads no more than the configured upload chunk from `SSHUploadSource`;
   269	3. loops on `writeSome` until each chunk is accepted, with total adapter-owned
   270	   queued bytes bounded by channel policy;
   271	4. drains stdout and stderr concurrently so remote output cannot deadlock stdin;
   272	5. sends EOF with `finishWriting`, waits for exit, and closes the channel;
   273	6. on source, write, timeout, exit-wait, or cancellation failure, resets only the
   274	   upload channel and preserves the connection unless the underlying error is
   275	   connection-fatal.
   276	
   277	Remote path quoting, checksum policy, atomic rename, and relay installation are
   278	higher-layer concerns. The SSH transport never logs the command, path, stdin,
   279	stdout, or stderr.
   280	
   281	## 8. Bounded read, write, EOF, reset, cancel, and close
   282	
   283	Each channel has independent read-side and write-side states:
   284	
   285	```text
   340	
   341	```text
   342	0 < adjustment
   343	remainingCreditBefore + adjustment <= maximumAdvertisedReceiveWindowBytes
   344	```
   345	
   346	An advertised window is never revoked. Memory pressure may withhold adjustment
   347	and influence policy for future channels, but cannot raise this channel's cap.
   348	The adapter MUST use the configured policy for direct and exec channels; the
   349	libssh2 direct helper's 2 MiB default and NIOSSH's audited 16 MiB target do not
   350	override it.
   351	
   352	`receiveWindow()` returns a consistent snapshot containing initial value, cap,
   353	remaining protocol credit, buffered unread bytes, delivered-but-not-yet-returned
   354	credit, adjustment count, and cumulative adjustment bytes. A typed
   355	`windowAdjusted(channel, before, amount, after, cap)` event makes each adjust
   356	independently observable without payload or endpoint data.
   357	
   358	## 10. Rekey and keepalive
   359	
   360	### 10.1 Rekey
   361	
   362	`SSHRekeyPolicy` supplies a positive protected-byte threshold per direction, a
   363	positive elapsed-time threshold, and a rekey timeout. Protected bytes are
   364	counted after SSH packet encoding and before socket write, or after socket read
   365	and before SSH packet decoding; TCP/IP overhead is excluded. Either direction
   366	reaching the byte threshold triggers client rekey. Time uses the injected
   367	monotonic clock from the last successful key exchange, independent of traffic.
   368	
   369	Client triggers are `byteThreshold`, `timeThreshold`, and explicit `test` or
   370	`manual`. Server `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` is `serverInitiated`. At most one KEX runs per
   371	connection. Concurrent triggers coalesce into that KEX while retaining a set of
   372	observed reasons. A successful KEX increments `keyExchangeGeneration`, resets
   373	protected-byte counters and the monotonic origin, and emits started/succeeded
   374	events. The explicit test trigger uses the same production path; no test-only
   375	engine symbol crosses the adapter boundary.
   376	
   377	Cancellation of a caller waiting on `requestRekey` does not attempt to roll back
   378	an already-started connection-global KEX; the call returns `cancelled` while the
   379	transport continues and reports the eventual result. Rekey timeout, protocol
   380	failure, or key validation failure is connection-fatal because crypto state is
   381	uncertain. Existing channels then fail with that lane-local connection error;
   382	cross-lane recovery belongs to the lane pool.
   383	
   384	During KEX, no payload is lost, duplicated, or reordered; read/write suspension
   385	is bounded as in section 8, and new opens wait as in section 5. The linked
   386	channel diagram is `TASK-260715-2ny6z4_channel-window-rekey-sequence.puml`.
   387	
   388	### 10.2 Keepalive
   389	
   390	`SSHKeepalivePolicy` supplies interval, reply timeout, and allowed consecutive
   391	misses. Automatic keepalive uses an SSH global request that requires a reply.
   392	Its schedule is based on monotonic time and is not reset merely by application
   393	payload; SSH liveness remains distinct from application traffic. A manual
   394	`sendKeepalive` exercises the same path and returns measured monotonic RTT.
   395	
   396	Only one keepalive is outstanding. A due keepalive during KEX is deferred until
   397	KEX succeeds without creating an unbounded queue. Success, timeout, and RTT are
   398	observable. Exhausting the miss allowance is connection-fatal. Keepalive never
   399	claims to detect physical path changes; that belongs to higher lifecycle code.
   400	
   401	## 11. Timeouts and cancellation points
   402	
   403	`SSHTimeoutPolicy` supplies separate values for DNS/endpoint resolution, TCP
   404	connect, initial KEX, host decision, credential lookup, authentication, channel
   405	open, write-credit wait, explicit rekey, keepalive reply, exec exit, upload, and
   406	graceful channel/transport close. Long-lived reads have no implicit idle timeout;
   407	a caller may supply a deadline/cancel its task. Final durations are out of scope.
   408	
   409	Every async operation checks cancellation before starting and immediately after
   410	each suspension/readiness wake. Required cancellable waits are: resolution, TCP
   411	connect/readiness, KEX, host policy, credential lookup, authentication, channel
   412	open, read, write credit, EOF flush, exec exit, upload-source read, rekey wait,
   413	keepalive reply, and graceful close wait.
   414	
   415	| Cancellation/timeout site | Required scope and cleanup |
   416	|---|---|
   417	| Resolve/TCP/KEX/host/auth during `connect` | Connection-fatal; close partial socket/session; no credential access after host rejection |
   418	| Channel open before remote ID | Fail only the open attempt |
   419	| Channel open after remote ID | Best-effort close partial channel, unregister, then fail attempt |
   420	| Read or write-credit wait | Cancel only that operation; channel remains usable when no bytes were accepted |
   421	| `finishWriting` | If EOF status is uncertain, reset that channel; never retry EOF blindly |
   422	| Exec wait/upload | Reset that exec channel; connection survives unless cause is connection-fatal |
   423	| Explicit rekey wait | Before start: no effect; after start: caller may leave, KEX continues |
   424	| Rekey timeout/failure | Connection-fatal |
   425	| Keepalive reply timeout | Count miss; connection-fatal only at configured miss limit |
   426	| Channel close timeout | Force-release that channel |
   427	| Transport close timeout | Force-release all remaining channels/session/socket; cleanup itself is not caller-cancellable |
   428	
   429	Cancellation and timeout are separate stable errors and counters. Adapters MUST
   430	NOT implement deterministic tests with sleeps or busy polling.
   431	
   432	## 12. Error taxonomy
   433	
   434	`SSHTransportError` contains only common data: stable `code`, `phase`, scope
   435	(`operation`, channel identity, or lane), retry disposition, and whether teardown
   520	limits are prohibited. An explicitly unsupported capability is a recorded red
   521	assertion, not a skip.
   522	
   523	Every row records candidate pin, adapter revision, target/device/OS, server
   524	fixture and revision, negotiated algorithms, policy values, traffic/duration,
   525	loss/latency when applicable, result, common error, metrics snapshot, and raw
   526	artifact location.
   527	
   528	| ID | Required rows and assertions |
   529	|---|---|
   530	| E-ALGO | Current OpenSSH Linux/macOS, documented older profiles, and real relux host; Ed25519, ECDSA, and RSA-SHA2 host/user fixtures as applicable; exact negotiated KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC; forbidden SHA-1/CBC never negotiated; unsupported combinations fail explicitly. |
   531	| E-HOSTAUTH | First-use-with-explicit-record, approved match, unknown reject, policy reject, changed-key stop, forbidden algorithm, credential unavailable, interaction required, signature reject, auth reject; spies prove raw bytes/algorithm/fingerprint reach policy before any credential/auth callback and rejection opens no channel. |
   532	| E-CHANNELS | Direct destination/originator wire correctness; exec request acceptance/rejection; simultaneous stdin/stdout/stderr; upload checksum through exec stdin with no SFTP call; early close, ordered EOF, both half-close directions, peer reset, local reset, cancel, idempotent close, exit status/signal/absent ordering, sibling independence. |
   533	| E-BACKPRESSURE | Write-call and aggregate queue caps; stalled socket makes `writeSome` suspend without polling or growth; partial-prefix retry is byte exact; cancellation before acceptance is zero; read stall never exceeds buffer cap. |
   534	| E-WINDOW | Separate direct and exec channels at 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and caller-supplied capped BDP profiles; initial wire window equals policy; remaining snapshot falls under withheld reads; adjustment follows delivered bytes; every adjustment stays under cap; channels do not share credit; aggregate gauges reconcile. |
   535	| E-REKEY | Deterministic byte threshold in each direction, fake-clock time threshold, explicit production-path trigger, server trigger, simultaneous/coalesced triggers, keepalive due during KEX, new open during KEX, active direct+exec channels, and at least 5 GiB mixed transfer; generation/keys change and content has no loss/duplication/reordering. |
   536	| E-KEEPALIVE | Fake-clock schedule independent of application traffic; one outstanding request; reply RTT; timeout/miss threshold; KEX deferral; connection failure only after configured allowance. |
   537	| E-CANCEL | Cancellation in resolution, TCP, KEX, host decision, credential lookup, auth, direct/exec open before and after remote ID, read, write credit, EOF, exec exit, upload source, rekey before/after start, keepalive, and close; 100 connect/cancel and channel/cancel cycles return tasks/channels/sessions/sockets/descriptors to baseline within injected deadlines. |
   538	| E-SCALE | Staged 100/250/500/1,000 independent channels plus concurrent direct+exec mix; report open/close latency, CPU, base/peak physical footprint, per-channel slope, allocator counts where available, queued/buffer/window totals, and descriptors; all configured bounds and lifecycle invariants hold. |
   539	| E-ERRORS | Every stable error maps to correct phase/scope/retry disposition; no candidate type/code leaks; channel-scoped faults preserve siblings; connection-scoped faults fail all channels once. |
   540	| E-METRICS-PRIVACY | Every counter/gauge/event reconciles with scripted activity and serialized order. Sentinel hostnames, endpoints, fingerprints, commands, paths, key material, and payloads are absent from logs, public errors, events, and snapshots. |
   541	| E-INJECTION | Same suite constructs both candidates through `SSHTransportFactory`; fake clock/readiness/policy/credential/observer are replaceable; ordinary core consumers cannot import or downcast to candidate types. |
   542	
   543	E-ALGO, E-HOSTAUTH, E-CHANNELS, E-SCALE, E-WINDOW, and E-REKEY are the named
